---
// Figure: how requests spread over items with Zipf-like popularity, as the
// share of requests received by the most popular x% of items. One control:
// the exponent s.
import SimFigure from '../shared/SimFigure.astro';
import Slider from '../shared/Slider.astro';
import Readout from '../shared/Readout.astro';
interface Props { number: number }
const { number } = Astro.props;
---
<SimFigure number={number} id="intro-popularity" title="How much traffic the most popular products receive" controls="none"
  >
  <Fragment slot="caption">Computed exactly from a Zipf-like distribution over 100,000 products: the product ranked k receives requests in proportion to 1/k<sup>s</sup>. The horizontal axis is logarithmic, from the top 0.01% (10 products) to all of them, so each step to the right is ten times as many products. Real traffic only approximates this shape; measure your own (see the text).</Fragment>
  <Slider slot="params" name="skew" label="Exponent s" min={0} max={1.4} step={0.01} value={1} />
  <Fragment slot="stats">
    <Readout name="top1" label="Requests for the top 1%" value="–" kind="client" />
    <Readout name="top10" label="Requests for the top 10%" value="–" kind="client" />
    <Readout name="for90" label="Products needed for 90% of requests" value="–" />
  </Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="text">
    <p>The products are sorted from most to least popular. The curve shows, for each share of the most popular products along the bottom, the share of all requests they receive. The bottom axis is logarithmic: 0.01%, 0.1%, 1%, 10% and 100% are evenly spaced. A dashed line shows even popularity for comparison. A cache that held exactly those products could serve at most that share of requests.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>s = 0: the curve lies on the dashed line; the most popular 10% of products receive 10% of requests.</li>
      <li>s = 0.64 and 0.83, the ends of the range Breslau and colleagues measured in web proxy traces: the top 1% receive 18% and 38% of requests, the top 10% receive 43% and 63%.</li>
      <li>s = 1: the curve is close to a straight line on this axis: each tenfold increase in products adds about the same share of requests. The top 1% receive 62% of requests and the top 10% receive 81%; 30% of the products cover 90% of requests.</li>
      <li>s = 1.2: the top 1% receive 85%, and 3% of products cover 90% of requests.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</SimFigure>
<script>
  import { bindParams, setReadouts } from '../shared/player.mjs';
  import { lineChart } from '../shared/chart.mjs';
  import { zipf } from '../shared/sim.mjs';
  import { itemsFor } from './model.mjs';

  const N = 100000;
  // x positions are log10 of the percentage of products, from 0.01% (10 products) to 100%,
  // so the top 1% and top 10% that the text discusses get as much room as the rest.
  const LOGS = Array.from({ length: 41 }, (_, i) => -2 + i / 10);
  const pct = (log: number) => Math.pow(10, log);
  const tick = (log: number) => (Number.isInteger(log) ? `${Number(pct(log).toPrecision(2))}%` : '');
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-figure-id="intro-popularity"]').forEach(root => {
    const chart = lineChart(root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-scene]')!, {
      label: 'Share of all requests received by the most popular products, against the share of products',
      height: 260,
      x: { label: 'Most popular products (log scale)', domain: [-2, 2], format: tick },
      y: { label: 'Share of all requests', domain: [0, 100], format: (v: number) => `${v}%` },
      series: [
        { id: 'skewed', label: 'With the chosen exponent', kind: 'client', dash: '' },
        { id: 'even', label: 'Even popularity (s = 0)', kind: 'neutral', dash: '5 4' },
      ],
    });
    chart.set('even', LOGS.map(x => [x, pct(x)]));
    const percent = (v: number) => (v >= 0.1 ? `${Math.round(v * 100)}%` : v >= 0.01 ? `${(v * 100).toFixed(1)}%` : `${(v * 100).toFixed(2)}%`);
    bindParams(root, params => {
      const skew = Number(params.skew);
      const z = zipf(N, skew);
      chart.set('skewed', LOGS.map(x => [x, z.topShare(Math.round((pct(x) / 100) * N)) * 100]));
      chart.render();
      setReadouts(root, { top1: percent(z.topShare(N / 100)), top10: percent(z.topShare(N / 10)), for90: percent(itemsFor(0.9, skew)) });
    });
  });
</script>
